p.src:
That capital constraint produces the next move. American Home grows rapidly, and Greenberg looks for other insurers with the same sort of fixable weakness. National Union is acquired and repaired in much the same way. New Hampshire follows. Then, in 1967, a holding company is placed over these firms and AIG is born. The order matters. AIG is not introduced as a brand in search of a theory. It is the organizational consequence of a problem-solving sequence: $\text{repair business model}
\Rightarrow
\text{improve underwriting}
\Rightarrow
\text{obtain reinsurance}
\Rightarrow
\text{solve capital bottleneck}
\Rightarrow
\text{create holding-company structure}.$

