That is the bridge to AIG Financial Products. Before the crisis, AIG had already expanded further into life insurance, consumer finance, thrift operations, and related financial services, supported by a huge asset base: $\text{assets to invest} \approx 1\text{ trillion dollars}.$ AIG Financial Products, in Greenberg’s telling, began as a successful derivatives business. It was hedged. It was monitored. The firm even maintained mirrored computer systems off-site so that transactions could be tracked independently. He summarizes the control principle in a phrase worth preserving: $\text{enterprise risk management}
=
\text{monitoring of market risk and credit risk across the firm}.$

