The cleanest schematic comparison is: $\begin{aligned}
\text{old rule:} \quad & \mathbf{1}_{\{\text{default}\}} \Rightarrow \text{payment},\\
\text{new rule:} \quad & \Delta P_{\mathrm{CDO}} < 0 \Rightarrow \text{collateral posting}.\end{aligned}$ That is the decisive contractual shift. Under the old regime, one waits for default. Under the new regime, one responds to a mark. In Greenberg’s language, the instrument did not have to default. It only had to lose value, and then the protection seller had to put up collateral.

