He then adds mark-to-market accounting as an amplifier: $V_t^{\text{book/held-to-maturity}}
\longrightarrow
V_t^{\text{market}}.$ The lecture’s claim is not that mark-to-market accounting invented bad assets. It is that, introduced at exactly the wrong moment, it forced severe writedowns and destroyed capital in ways Greenberg sees as artificial. Again, that is his diagnosis, but it fits the overall structure of his argument: several individually dangerous elements came together at once.

