p.src:
His answer is explicit. First, return the CDS contract to a default-based trigger: $\text{CDS should respond only to default, not merely to a temporary fall in market value.}$ Second, create exchange trading so that there is genuine price discovery: $\text{exchange trading}
\Rightarrow
\text{price discovery}
\Rightarrow
\text{more disciplined collateral and regulation.}$ Third, if the instrument functions as insurance, regulate it as insurance and require reserves. The logic is that price discovery, reserve requirements, and a default trigger all work in the same direction: they break the link between temporary marks and system-wide liquidity panic.

